# 📝 Building a To-Do List App with JavaScript

A to-do list app is one of the best beginner projects to learn JavaScript! Here's everything you need to get started.

---

## 🔧 What You'll Need

- **HTML** – for the structure (input field, buttons, task list)
- **CSS** – for styling
- **Vanilla JavaScript** – for the logic (no frameworks required!)

---

## 🧱 Core Features to Build

| Feature | How It Works |
|---|---|
| **Add a task** | Grab input value, create a new `<li>` element, append it to a `<ul>` |
| **Mark as complete** | Toggle a CSS class (e.g., `line-through`) on click |
| **Delete a task** | Remove the `<li>` element from the DOM |
| **Edit a task** | Replace the text content with an input field, then save |
| **Persist data** | Use `localStorage` so tasks survive a page refresh |

---

## 📐 Basic Structure

**HTML:**
```html
<input type="text" id="taskInput" placeholder="Add a new task...">
<button id="addBtn">Add</button>
<ul id="taskList"></ul>
```

**JavaScript (key concepts):**
```javascript
// 1. Select DOM elements
const input = document.getElementById('taskInput');
const addBtn = document.getElementById('addBtn');
const taskList = document.getElementById('taskList');

// 2. Add task on button click
addBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  if (input.value.trim() === '') return; // prevent empty tasks
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.textContent = input.value;
  taskList.appendChild(li);
  saveTasks();
  input.value = '';
});

// 3. Toggle complete / delete on click
taskList.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  if (e.target.tagName === 'LI') {
    e.target.classList.toggle('completed');
    saveTasks();
  }
});

// 4. Save to localStorage
function saveTasks() {
  localStorage.setItem('tasks', taskList.innerHTML);
}

// 5. Load on page refresh
window.addEventListener('load', () => {
  taskList.innerHTML = localStorage.getItem('tasks') || '';
});
```

